Here is a simplified pseudocode description of the Renderman Shading Language (RSL) "rt" algorithm, including refraction:

```pseudocode
void rt(  
  in vector3 Pc // camera position
  in vector3 Pt // point to shade
  in vector3 Nt // normal at Pt
  in color3 Kd // diffuse color
  in color3 Ks // specular color
  in float N // normal distribution
  in float F0 // roughness
  in float NoV // angle between normal and view vector
  in float NoL // angle between normal and light vector
  in color3 L // light color
  in color3 Cs // material color
  out color3 result // output color
) {

  // compute view vector from camera to Pt
  vector3 V = normalize(Pt - Pc);

  // compute light vector from Pt to light position (assuming Lambertian lighting)
  vector3 L = normalize(L - pt);

  // compute half-way vector (for specular term)
  vector3 H = normalize(V + L);

  // compute outgoing radiance (result)
  result = Cs * (Kd * (cos(NoL) * cos(NoL) * NoV) +
                 Ks * pow(cos(NoH), (1/F0) * (1 + N)));

  // handle refraction (Snell's law)
  float ni = 1.0; // index of refraction (1.0 for air)
  float nt = 1.5; // index of refraction (1.5 for water)
  float eta = 0.0; // refractive error (0.0 for no refraction)

  if (Nt · L > 0) { // if light is on the same side as the normal
    eta = ni / nt;
    float cosi = dot(Nt, V); // cosine of incidence angle
    float cos2t = 1 - eta * eta * (1 - cosi * cosi);

    if (cos2t < 0) { // total internal reflection
      result *= 0.0;
    } else {
      // compute refraction (Fresnel equation)
      float R = fresnel(eta, cosi);

      // compute transmitted intensity
      color3 Ti = Cs * R;

      // integrate transmitted intensity over hemisphere
      Ti *= 0.5;

      // add transmitted intensity to result
      result += Ti;
    }
  }

  return result;
}
```

Note that this is a highly simplified version of the full RT algorithm, and in practice, you would need to handle many more cases, such as:

* Handling multiple light sources
* Using more accurate lighting models (e.g. physically-based rendering)
* Implementing more complex shading models (e.g. anisotropic scattering)
* Optimizing the algorithm for performance

